  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 120 AM CDT Sat Jun 20 2026\n\nMedium chance of showers and thunderstorms continues today. Some \nstorms may produce heavy rainfall possibly leading to localized \nflooding, especially low lying areas, creeks, streams and urban \nareas. This is due to efficient heavy rainfall processes with a \nfairly saturated environment. PWATs of 2.0-2.2 inches are forecast \nto remain across the region and interact with a mid level low draped \nacross the western portions of the CWA. In addition, the environment \nwill be moderately unstable with little to no CIN. The caveat will \nbe the mid level high pressure centered over the northwest gulf. \nSome models suggest the high pressure will nudge westward, bringing \ndrier mid levels to the eastern portions of the CWA today. Timing of \nthe drier air will determine whether the Coastal Bend and Victoria \nCrossroads will get heavy rain or not. These areas have saturated \nsoil from previous rains and will be prone to flash flooding. But if \nthe drier mid levels move across the area early enough, then rain \nchances and rainfall amounts will be lower. Rain chances are \nexpected to decrease this evening.\n\nModels are in fair agreement that the mid level high will become \nmore prominent for the upcoming week, leading to very low (<5%) \nrain chances. Can not rule out a few weak showers along the sea \nbreeze each afternoon, as low level moisture will remain in place.\n\nThe low level moisture combined with highs in the 90s to near 100 \nout west will lead to heat indices 105-113 across the region for \nSunday and Monday and Heat Advisories may be needed for portions of \nS TX. A moderate risk of heat related impacts can be expected \nthrough the period.\n\nThe rip current risk will be moderate today due to 8 second swell \nperiods. Minor Coastal Flooding is not anticipated due to lower \nastronomical tides and a S-SE wind direction over the gulf waters.",
